  "body": "Closes #188.\n\n## Problem\n\n`clients setup` could only take an existing token through `--token` in argv (visible in shell history and process listings), had no way to run against an isolated configuration root, could echo a token back through router error bodies, and releases shipped Linux archives only — so macOS users had no supported install path and nothing exercised the client lifecycle on macOS.\n\n## What changed\n\n- **Darwin release assets.** `publish-release-artifacts` is now a per-platform matrix (`linux-amd64`, `linux-arm64`, `darwin-arm64`, `darwin-amd64`), each leg cross-building with `--target`, packaging per-platform `.tar.gz` / `.cdx.json` / `.sha256` names, and falling back to `shasum -a 256` where GNU `sha256sum` is absent. Both Darwin slices build on the Apple Silicon runner, so the x86_64 asset does not depend on the shrinking Intel macOS runner pool.\n- **Secret-safe token input.** `clients setup` gained `--token-stdin` and reads `LINK_ASSISTANT_ROUTER_TOKEN` (alias `LINK_ASSISTANT_TOKEN`). Precedence is `--token` → `--token-stdin` → env; `--token`/`--token-stdin` are mutually exclusive; a non-router token is rejected before anything is written, naming the checked inputs without echoing the value.\n- **Redacted diagnostics.** `ClientError::message` is now the single choke point and runs every client diagnostic — including router error bodies, IO, and serde errors — through `login_url::redact_secrets`; `client_command::failed` redacts as well.\n- **Isolated home.** `clients --home DIR` resolves every client config root under `DIR` and ignores the clients' override variables *and* ambient token variables, so automation can prove setup → doctor → launch → remove without touching real user settings.\n- **macOS release lifecycle.** New `verify-macos-client-lifecycle` job downloads the published `darwin-arm64` asset, verifies checksums, runs the shipped binaries natively, opens an `ssh -L` forward to a remote router, and runs the host CLI lifecycle test against it. It skips cleanly when the SSH secrets are absent.\n\n## Reproduction and tests\n\nBefore this change, `link-assistant-router clients setup codex --token-stdin` failed with an unknown-argument error, and a router 401 body quoting the token was printed verbatim.\n\nNew automated coverage:\n\n- `tests/clients_cli_test.rs`: token accepted on stdin; token accepted from the documented env var; non-router token rejected from every input with exit code 2 and no echo; full isolated-home lifecycle leaves the real configuration untouched; an isolated home ignores a token variable exported in the calling shell; a router error body that echoes the token is redacted (`la_sk_[redacted]` present, `la_sk_leaky` absent).\n- `tests/host_client_lifecycle_test.rs` (new, opt-in via `ROUTER_HOST_CLI_TESTS=1` + `ROUTER_HOST_CLI_URL` + `ROUTER_HOST_CLI_TOKEN`): runs the shipped binaries for codex, claude-code, opencode, qwen-code, and agent through setup → show → doctor → optional launch → remove against a real router, asserting the token never appears in output and the managed credential file is deleted.\n- `tests/release_workflow_test.rs`: asserts the four build targets, per-platform asset naming, the `shasum` fallback, and the macOS lifecycle job with its SSH forward.\n\n`cargo fmt`, `cargo clippy --locked --all-targets --all-features`, and `cargo test` pass locally.\n",
